## Classic Red and Green Christmas Nails

Red and green earned their place as the default for a reason. They just need better execution than a flat coat straight from the bottle.

A true classic doesn't have to mean plain. Cherry red with a glossy top coat still looks polished a decade from now, and forest green with a matte finish avoids the candy-cane-adjacent look that cheaper polishes tend toward. The trick with <strong>christmas nails</strong> in these two shades is picking one dominant color rather than splitting the set evenly between them, which tends to read busier than intended. A full red set with one green accent nail, or vice versa, holds together far better than alternating nail by nail.

<strong>French tip</strong> is a nail technique where the tip of the nail is painted a contrasting color, traditionally white, while the base stays a neutral or sheer shade. Applied to the holiday palette, a <a href='/blog/christmas-nails/french-tip-christmas-nails'>french tip christmas nails</a> look swaps the classic white tip for red or green against a nude or sheer pink base, which softens the boldness of a full-color set while keeping it unmistakably festive. It also happens to be one of the easier techniques to execute at home, since the tip line is more forgiving than a full-nail application.

If you want the fully saturated version rather than an accent approach, the dedicated <a href='/blog/christmas-nails/red-christmas-nails'>red christmas nails</a> and <a href='/blog/christmas-nails/green-christmas-nails'>green christmas nails</a> guides each cover finish options, from cream to jelly to metallic, in more depth than a single shared section can.

